        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 73,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:08:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62043,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 73,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Presentation is usually with symptoms attributable to an ovarian mass. Hormonal manifestations are usually absent.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:08:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62044,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 73,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are rare. They have occurred in adults with ages ranging from 21 to 83&nbsp;years {7483017; 14668550; 20664453; 26509912; 27513075; 30676431}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:08:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62045,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 73,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:08:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62046,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 73,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The exact pathogenesis is uncertain, but signet-ring stromal tumour probably represents a degenerative cytoplasmic change within a stromal tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:08:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62047,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 73,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Signet-ring stromal tumour is typically unilateral, but rare bilateral cases have been reported {30676431; 20664453}. The cut surface is usually solid, whitish-yellow, and firm.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:08:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62048,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 73,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Variable numbers of signet-ring cells are present in a background stromal component, which often resembles cellular fibroma {7483017; 14668550}. The nuclei are eccentrically located, small, uniform, and bland. Small nucleoli and occasional nuclear grooves may be present. Mitotic figures are rare or absent. The signet-ring cells contain a single large cytoplasmic vacuole, which does not contain mucin, glycogen, or lipid. Eosinophilic hyaline globules may be present. A collision tumour with components of signet-ring stromal tumour and steroid cell tumour has been reported {27513075}. PAS and mucin histochemical stains are negative. Immunohistochemically, the tumour cells are usually positive for calretinin, SF1, and SMA. They may be focally positive for pancytokeratin and are typically negative for inhibin and EMA. Nuclear &beta;-catenin and cyclin&nbsp;D1 expression, along with CD10 immunoreactivity, has been reported {26509912}. Abnormalities of <em>CTNNB1</em> have been reported in occasional cases {26509912}. Distinction from metastatic signet-ring cell carcinoma is based on negative EMA and absence of intracytoplasmic mucin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:08:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62049,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 73,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:08:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
